<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Sohawal State</h1><p class="st-meta">1550 CE – 1950 CE (approx.) · capital Sohawal</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Baghel Rajput state of Baghelkhand, founded by a younger son of the Rewa ruling house and later itself split into smaller holdings.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Sohawal was founded around 1550 by Fateh Singh, a younger son of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghel">Baghel</a> ruler of Rewa, who received lands near the Satna river as his share. Like many cadet branches in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/rewa-state">Baghelkhand</a>, it became a separate principality while acknowledging kinship with Rewa State.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Raja ruled a small agrarian territory of villages and jungle. Over time the state lost much of its territory to relatives and neighbours. In the British period Sohawal was a non-salute state under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghelkhand-agency">Baghelkhand Agency</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Its last ruler acceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1947, and in 1948 Sohawal was merged into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/vindhya-pradesh">Vindhya Pradesh</a>, which itself became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a> in 1956. Its lands lie in today's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satna-district">Satna district</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sohawal-state.jpg" alt="Sohawal State" width="320" height="226"><figcaption>Image: Xufanc · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sohawal_State-flag.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1550 CE – 1950 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sohawal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Fateh Singh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bagheli</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>India</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sohawal</dd></dl></aside></div>
